1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bd3 Na6 { [%csl Gd5,Gd3][%cal Ga6b4,Gb4d5,Gb4d3] } (6... Nc6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] In this position, white has to choose between e5 and castles, which will be a choice for many moves in the Weiss variation. Black can choose between castles and playing e5 himself, so white has to keep that in mind. } 7. O-O (7. e5 dxe5 8. fxe5 (8. dxe5 Nd5 9. Bd2 Ncb4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] In these positions, white has strong bishops but black has very active knights. White still needs to cast though, and black has an easy time developing. } (9... Nb6)) 8... Nh5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Here, white has central control and the possibility for a kingside push and potentially a queenside castle. }) 7... e5 8. fxe5 dxe5 9. d5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Here, white has blocked off both black's dark-squared bishop and his light-squared bishop. White has achieved d5 to kick the knight but white will have a harder time attacking. } { [%cal Gd5c6,Gg7e5,Gd3f5] } 9... Nd4 10. Nxe5 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Bxe5 12. c3 Nf5 13. Qf3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Here, white's center is worse but the position is pretty neutral. Black's queen has some potential on h4 but white has a battery on the f file once it opens up. } { [%csl Gf1,Gf3][%cal Gd8h4,Gf1f8,Gf3f8] }) 7. e5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] I like this over castles because white usually retains the initiative. } (7. O-O c5 8. d5 Bg4 9. Bc4 Nc7 { [%cal Ge7e6,Gd5e6,Gc7e6] } 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Qxf3 e6 12. dxe6 fxe6 (12... Nxe6 13. Qd3 Nd4 14. Nd5 Nxd5 15. Bxd5 Nc6) 13. Be3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Here, both sides have a presence in the center but it is sort of a liability for both. White tries to double up rooks on the d file and black tries to dislodge the defenders of d5 so he can push and eliminate that weakness. } { [%cal Ga1d1,Gd1d2,Gf1d1,Gb7b5,Gc4b3,Gd6d5,Ge3c5] }) 7... Nd7 8. Be3 c5 (8... Nb4 { [%csl Gd3] } 9. O-O Nxd3 10. Qxd3 c6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Here, white has a much strong center and only needs to develop rooks to complete development. Although white lost their light-squared bishop, they have a lot more potential for attack. }) 9. Bxa6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Here, white's center is crumbling and black has the bishop pair, but white has superior development. } *


