[StudyName "Austrian Attack"]
[ChapterName "Fischer v. Benko, 1963/64 US Championship"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/BTujsxIs/DATay7SM"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Funnichessman335"]
[UTCDate "2026.11.18"]
[UTCTime "18:35:17"]
[Orientation "white"]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 Nf6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bd3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is the Weiss Variation. } 6... Bg4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is an inaccuracy. Black should play Nf6 and after 0-0, then Bg4. This way, if white plays h3, he weakens his kingside further and black has counterplay on the open king. } (6... Nc6 7. O-O Bg4 8. e5 dxe5 9. dxe5) 7. h3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This move now works because white can always castle queenside. } 7... Bxf3 8. Qxf3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This just gave white a glorious attacking set up where the bishops are coordinated and every one of white's pawns is ready to advance. } { [%cal Gd4d5,Ge4e5,Gf4f5,Gg2g4,Gh3h4,Gc1h6,Gd3h7,Gc3d5,Gc3e4] } 8... Nc6 9. Be3 e5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Because white did not play e5, it allows black to attempt to grab space in the center. } 10. dxe5 dxe5 11. f5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Normally, breaking with the f pawn in the Austrian Attack is worse than the e pawn, but here, white can open lines to black's king and potentially advance with the other kingside pawns. Note that fxe5 is a very weakening move and gives black a perfect knight. } { [%cal Gf5g6,Gf3f5,Gg2g4] } 11... gxf5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is just a horrendous move on Benko's part. It opens the king's file and gives Bobby all the attacking chances he could ask for. } (11... Nd4 12. Qf2 b5 13. g4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is an interesting line where black forces white back and seeks counterplay on the queenside. Although b5 is a pawn sacrifice, it gives black a lot of attacking chances. }) 12. Qxf5 Nd4 13. Qf2 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Not trading bishop for knight to keep the queen on f5 because white hopes to trade his dark square bishop for the bishop on g7, the main defender of black's king. } 13... Ne8 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Preparing f5. } 14. O-O { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Not only does this prevent f5, it also gives white a nice queen rook battery and control over the f file once the queen moves. } 14... Nd6 15. Qg3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Here Bobby begins his queen walk to get the queen to the optimal square. } 15... Kh8 16. Qg4 c6 17. Qh5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Here white's idea is to play e5 and threaten h7 and also potentially use the dark-squared bishop to coordinate an attack/remove the main defender on g7. } { [%cal Gd3h7,Ge3h6,Gh5h7] } 17... Qe8 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is a losing blunder by Benko. Bobby found the winning position, which is why he walked his queen to h5. } { [%csl Gf6] } (17... Ne6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is black's best hope for defense. } { [%cal Ge6f4] }) 18. Bxd4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] He either goes up a piece or gets to play e5. Either way, black is lost. } 18... exd4 19. Rf6 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } (19. e5 f5) 19... Kg8 (19... Bxf6 20. e5 Ne4 21. Bxe4 h6 22. Qxh6+ Kg8 23. Qh7#) (19... dxc3 20. e5 Ne4 21. Bxe4 Bh6 22. Qxh6 Rg8 23. Qxh7#) (19... h6 20. e5 Kg8 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black is not immediately getting mated here, but it is still completely losing. You can never take the rook because of Qxh6. }) 20. e5 h6 21. Ne2 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Bobby takes his time. There is no counterplay for black and he is just a piece down. Here black resigns. A crushing victory for Bobby and on his way to going 11/11 at the US Championship. } { [%cal Ge5f6,Gg7f6,Gh5g4] } *


