1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bd3 Na6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Prepares c5. } { [%cal Gc7c5] } 7. O-O c5 8. d5 Nc7 9. Qe1 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Placing the queen on a nice file and threatening to gain space with e5 if a knight moves. } 9... e6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is a mistake on black's part. Instead of responding in the center, black should counterattack on the queenside with the immediate b5. } (9... b5 10. Bxb5 Nxe4 11. Qxe4 Bxc3 12. bxc3 (12. Bc6 Bf6 13. Bxa8 Bf5 14. Qc4 Qxa8 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is a better line where white can preserve his pawn structure but still, his center is much worse. e6 is not the right way to undermine white's center. }) 12... Nxb5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black has neutralized white's attack and his center is crumbling. White's pawn structure is hideous. }) 10. dxe6 fxe6 11. e5 Nfd5 { [%cal Gd5f4] } (11... dxe5 12. fxe5 Nh5 13. Bg5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White's better. }) 12. Ne4 Nxf4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black should play Nb4 to win the light-squared bishop. This series of trades only favors white. } (12... Nb4 13. Nxd6 Nxd3 14. cxd3 Ne8 15. Nxc8 Rxc8) 13. Bxf4 Rxf4 14. Nxd6 Nd5 15. Qg3 Qf8 16. Rae1 Ne7 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Too passive. } 17. Ng5 Rxf1+ { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black should not trade rooks here. It only gets rid of a defender, but white brings another attacker in. } 18. Rxf1 Nf5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This sets up the winning combination that Wesley found. } 19. Bxf5 gxf5 (19... exf5 20. Qb3+ c4 21. Qxc4+ Be6 22. Qxe6+ Kh8 23. Ndf7+ Kg8 24. Nh6+ Kh8 25. Ngf7+ Qxf7 26. Nxf7+ Kg8 27. Nh6+ Kh8 28. Qg8+ Rxg8 29. Nf7#) 20. Rxf5 { So, Wesley - Mahjoob, Morteza, 1-0, Asia-ch 6th, 2007, https://lichess.org/P9OvGOZg } { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Bam! } 20... exf5 21. Qb3+ Kh8 22. Ndf7+ Kg8 23. Nh6+ Kh8 24. Ngf7+ Qxf7 25. Nxf7+ Kg8 26. Nh6+ Kf8 27. Qf7# *


