1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 Nf6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be3 b6 7. Qd2 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Battery. } { [%cal Ge3h6,Gd2h6] } 7... c5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black has achieved their goal of challenging white's center at the expense of the light squares. } 8. O-O-O { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Achieving a nice battery on the important e file. } 8... cxd4 9. Bxd4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White gains a bishop on the strong diagonal. } 9... Nc6 10. Bxf6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] An interesting move. Polgar seeks to remove one of the main defenders of black's king and start a push on the kingside. } 10... Bxf6 11. h4 Bg4 12. h5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Trying to open up the files to black's king. } 12... Bxh5 13. Rxh5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] An awesome rook sacrifice to gain open files to black's king. } 13... gxh5 14. Qd5 Rc8 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black has to give up the h5 pawn and allows white's queen closer to their own king unless they want to lose their knight. } (14... Bxc3 15. bxc3 Qc8 16. Qxh5 Qe6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This variation is better for black because it allows black's queen to participate in the defense. Black is just up an exchange. }) 15. Qxh5 Bg7 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Adding defense to the open g file and stopping checks. } 16. e5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] An important idea in this position, to break open the center and allow the bishop access to h7. } 16... Qe8 17. Qh3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] ? } 17... h6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Stopping h7 weaknesses and preventing Ng5. } 18. Bd3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Targeting the h7 square. } (18. Nd5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is the engine's recommendation. I don't like it because although it gains a strong central knight, it does nothing to attack the king. }) 18... Nb4 19. Be4 e6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black plays this to hope for exd6. } (19... Rc4 20. Qf5 Rxe4 21. Qxe4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] The engine wants black to exchange for the light-squared bishop at all costs to stop Qh7 ideas. White is still winning here. }) 20. f5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] A winning move. Black's kingside is feeling the pressure, and his only hope is to gain some relief with Rxc3. } (20. exd6 Rxc3 21. bxc3 Nxa2+ 22. Kd2 Nxc3 23. Bd3 Nxd1) 20... Rxc3 21. f6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Here, black MUST play Qa4 to threaten white. Other moves hang mate. } 21... Qb5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] The losing blunder. Black has a few spite checks and as long as white doesn't screw up, it's mate. } (21... Qa4 22. bxc3 Nxa2+ 23. Kb2 Qxe4 24. fxg7 Rc8 (24... Kxg7 25. Rd4 Qe3 26. Rg4+ Kh8 27. Kxa2) 25. Qxh6 Qh7 26. Qxh7+ Kxh7) 22. Qg3 { Polgar, Judit - Smirin, Ilia, 1-0, Istanbul ol (Men) 34th, 2000, https://lichess.org/0lHOqvG1 } { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black has a few spite checks, but it's all over. } *


