1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. e5 dxe5 8. fxe5 Nh5 9. Be3 Bg4 10. Be2 f6 11. exf6 exf6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black's first mistake. He should take with the knight to bring it back into the game. } 12. O-O f5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Prepares f4 to kick the dark-squared bishop and claim space. } 13. Qd2 f4 14. Bf2 Qd7 15. Rfe1 Rae8 16. Rad1 Kh8 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is a very principled position. Both sides have played without many mistakes and have completed development. } 17. d5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] An interesting choice. } 17... Bxf3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is the best way for black to go about this, placing the knight on the strong e5 square. } 18. Bxf3 Ne5 19. Ne4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White also places his knight on a strong central square. } 19... Qf5 20. Nc5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White brings his knight closer to support his d pawn but allows an open g file and loses the bishop pair. } 20... Nxf3+ 21. gxf3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Instead of trading rooks and giving white the e file, black should continue to pressure white's king. } 21... Rxe1+ 22. Rxe1 h6 23. Ne6 Rf7 24. Bxa7 b6 25. c4 Be5 26. c5 bxc5 27. Bxc5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White traded off a pawn and brought his bishop to an important supporting square and it cannot be challenged without black losing a defender as well. Instead of winning the overextended pawn, it was converted to a strong passed pawn. } 27... Kh7 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White prevents any back rank checks. } 28. a4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White threatens to push his other passed pawn to victory. } 28... Ng7 29. Qe2 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White threatens black's bishop. } 29... Bd6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black hopes to trade and blockade the d pawn so he can focus on the a and b pawns. } 30. Bd4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White gives up his strong d pawn hoping to win something on g7, but black is safe to take. } 30... Bb4 (30... Qxd5 31. Qe4 Qxe4 32. Rxe4 Nxe6 33. Rxe6) 31. Rd1 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Putting a rook behind an advanced pawn. } 31... Re7 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is a losing blunder. White can simplify and be up an exchange with a three pawns that are threatening to queen. } 32. Bxg7 { Shirov, Alexei - Nikolic, Predrag, 1-0, Amber-blindfold 08th, 1999, https://lichess.org/3leMQfTK } { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Pawns are too strong here. } { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gd5d6,Gc7d6,Ga4a8,Gb2b8,Gc7b6,Gd5d8,Gb2b6] } *


