1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 a6 5. a4 Nc6 6. Be3 e6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black tries for a small center to prevent any pawn advances. } 7. Nf3 Nge7 8. Bd3 f5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Weird f5 system. } 9. O-O { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White should advance the e pawn to claim more space. } (9. e5) 9... fxe4 10. Bxe4 O-O 11. Qd2 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White wants to trade her bishop for the bishop on g7 and start a kingside attack because of the open f file. } { [%cal Ge3h6,Gd2h6] } 11... Nf5 12. Bf2 Qf6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black should play d5 here to kick the bishop back and finally claim space in the center. } 13. d5 exd5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Taking is a mistake because it gives white a tempo gain and the e file for her rook all the space she could want. } 14. Nxd5 Qf7 (14... Qd8 15. Rae1) 15. Rae1 (15. Ng5 Qd7 16. Rae1 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is the engine's line because it improves the position of white's knight with tempo and white has the potential for a devastating kingside attack. }) 15... Bd7 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] REALLY bad. } 16. Nxc7 (16. Ng5 Qe8 17. Bxf5 Qd8 18. Be6+ Bxe6 19. Nxe6) 16... Rac8 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Another blunder. Black needs to get the queen and king off the same diagonal to avoid this, and now white is up an insurmountable amount of material. } 17. Bd5 Rxc7 18. Bxf7+ Rxf7 19. Ng5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black is not keeping this rook either. Here, black resigned. } *


