1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Nf3 c5 6. Bb5+ Bd7 7. e5 Ng4 8. e6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] An interesting move with some fun sidelines. } 8... Bxb5 9. exf7+ Kf8 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Taking the pawn, although tempting, is also dangerous for black and white can put black's king in the corner. } (9... Kxf7 10. Ng5+ Kg8 11. Nxb5) 10. Nxb5 Qa5+ 11. Nc3 Nc6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Black should trade here first to get the knight away from f3, so black can take the f7 pawn. This is asking for d5. } 12. d5 Nd4 13. Ng5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is actually not a great move. White should castle to put the rook behind his pawns and escape the pin. } 13... Nh6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is a mistake. Not only is the knight now solid on g5 but it retreated to a worse square. } (13... h6 14. Ne6+ (14. Ne4 Nf6 15. O-O) 14... Nxe6 15. dxe6 Bxc3+ 16. bxc3 Qxc3+ 17. Bd2) 14. O-O Nxf7 15. Nce4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] White ignores the pawn and focuses on their attack. } 15... h6 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is a bad move because it walks into the sequence that follows. } 16. Nxf7 Kxf7 17. c3 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Here, it's better to just let go of the knight. } 17... Nb5 18. Qg4 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] Now there are all sorts of threats of f5. } 18... Nc7 19. f5 { [%anno "hexachess", hexachess] This is just a crushing blow from white. Black is not getting away from this unscathed. Here, black resigned. } *


